  "profile": {
    "video_id": "iron-snail:cyAzm8O3RwY",
    "purpose": "A review of the Red Wing 3335 boots, covering their leather and waxed-canvas uppers, sole, a standing-in-water test, fit, and how their stitch-down construction compares with Goodyear-welted Red Wings like the 875 and 1907.",
    "video_type": "review",
    "thesis": "The creator explains why the Red Wing 3335 is his favourite boot over the 875 and 1907 and other boots. He also argues that its single-stitch stitch-down construction doesn't give it much real edge over a well-made Goodyear welt.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The 3335 combines Red Wing's Copper Rough and Tough pull-up leather with Martexin waxed canvas from Fairfield Textiles in New Jersey.",
      "It uses a Vibram mini lug sole rather than the crepe soles on the 875 and 1907.",
      "The video explains the main ways of joining upper to sole: Blake stitch, cementing, Goodyear welting and stitch-down.",
      "In the water test the boots lasted about 30 seconds fully submerged before the left one leaked.",
      "Fit is described as true to size but slim and tapered, so the toes feel tight with thick wool socks.",
      "The 3335 uses a single stitch rather than the double stitch some stitch-down boots use, so its advantage over Goodyear welts is modest.",
      "Red Wing uses a Puritan machine with triple stitching and wax-dipped thread to help seal out water."
    ],
    "outline": [
